---
// PRIVACY POLICY — for musacad.org and the Musa CAD application. Every statement
// here is checked against the code: the site (src/scripts/github-live.ts is its
// only network call and sessionStorage its only storage) and the engine
// (src/ui/update_checker.cpp, update_installer.cpp, dwg_installer.cpp).
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, MAINTAINER } from '../data/site';

const title = 'Privacy Policy — Musa CAD App and Website';
const description =
  'Musa CAD has no telemetry, no analytics and no account, and the website sets no cookies. What the app and musacad.org do send, to whom, and how to turn it off.';
const crumbs = [{ name: 'Privacy policy', path: '/privacy/' }];
const UPDATED = '2026-10-04';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const mail = `mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`;
---

<Page {title} {description} {crumbs}>
  <PageHeader eyebrow="Privacy" title="Privacy policy" {crumbs} updated={UPDATED}>
    <p>
      The short version: Musa CAD doesn’t track you. The app has no telemetry, no analytics and no
      account, and your drawings never leave your computer. This website sets no cookies and runs
      no analytics. Below is everything that does happen, in plain words.
    </p>
  </PageHeader>

  <article class="prose-doc mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 pb-28 sm:px-8">
    <h2 id="who">Who I am</h2>
    <p>
      I’m {MAINTAINER}. I make Musa CAD and run this website, musacad.org. When this policy says
      “I” or “me”, that’s who it means. You can reach me about anything here at
      <a href={mail}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="app">The Musa CAD application</h2>
    <h3 id="app-nothing">What it doesn’t do</h3>
    <p>
      Musa CAD has no account, no sign-in, no telemetry, no usage analytics, no crash reporting
      and no ads. It never uploads your drawings. Its settings — your preferences, where your DWG
      converter is installed, when it last looked for updates — are kept in an ordinary settings
      file on your computer.
    </p>

    <h3 id="update-check">The update check</h3>
    <p>
      The one thing Musa CAD does over the network on its own is look for a new version, at most
      once a day:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        It sends a single HTTPS request to GitHub (<code>api.github.com</code>) — or to Flathub
        (<code>flathub.org</code>) if you installed the Flatpak — asking for the latest release.
      </li>
      <li>
        The request carries nothing about you or your drawings. Its user agent names only the
        program and its version, for example <code>MusaCAD/0.6.0 (update check)</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Like any request on the internet, it shows GitHub or Flathub your IP address. They handle
        that under their own privacy policies; I don’t receive it.
      </li>
      <li>
        It’s on by default in the packaged builds and off in builds you compile yourself. Switch
        it off any time in Options (<code>OP</code>), or set the environment variable
        <code>MUSACAD_NO_UPDATE_CHECK</code>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="downloads-in-app">Downloads you ask for</h3>
    <p>Two features fetch files from the internet, and only when you choose to use them:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Updating on Windows.</strong> When you accept an update, Musa CAD downloads the
        new installer and its SHA-256 checksum from the project’s GitHub releases.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>DWG Setup.</strong> When you ask it to fetch the ODA File Converter, Musa CAD
        downloads it from the Open Design Alliance (<code>opendesign.com</code>). The converter is
        their program, under their terms, and it runs on your computer.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="website">This website</h2>
    <h3 id="no-cookies">No cookies, no analytics</h3>
    <p>
      musacad.org sets no cookies and uses no analytics, advertising or tracking of any kind.
      Its fonts are served from this site, not from a font service.
    </p>

    <h3 id="hosting">Hosting</h3>
    <p>
      The site is hosted on GitHub Pages. To deliver pages and keep the service secure, GitHub
      processes the usual details of each request — your IP address, browser and the page you
      asked for — under the
      <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub Privacy Statement</a>.
      GitHub Pages doesn’t give me visitor logs.
    </p>

    <h3 id="live-version">The live version number</h3>
    <p>
      So that the download buttons always point at the newest release, your browser asks
      GitHub’s public API (<code>api.github.com</code>) for the current version and star count.
      GitHub sees that request like any other. The answer is kept for 15 minutes in your
      browser’s session storage — not a cookie, never sent anywhere, and gone when you close
      the tab.
    </p>

    <h3 id="third-party-sites">Downloads and other sites</h3>
    <p>
      Downloads come from GitHub releases or Flathub, and links to other sites take you to
      services with their own privacy policies. Once you follow a link, this policy no longer
      covers what happens there.
    </p>

    <h2 id="donations">Donations</h2>
    <p>
      If you <a href={`${base}donate/`}>donate</a>, the payment goes through the service you pick —
      your UPI app, PayPal or Ko-fi — under that service’s privacy policy. It shares with me what
      it shares with any recipient, typically your name, the amount and any message you add. I
      use that only to keep my own records and to thank you, and I never sell or share it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="email">If you email me</h2>
    <p>
      I get your email address and whatever you write. I use it to reply, keep it only as long
      as the conversation needs, and never add you to a mailing list or pass it on.
    </p>

    <h2 id="rights">Your choices and rights</h2>
    <p>
      Since I don’t collect personal data through the app or the site, there’s usually nothing to
      look up. For anything I do hold — emails, donation records — you can ask me to show it to
      you, correct it or delete it by writing to <a href={mail}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>. You also
      have whatever rights the data-protection law where you live gives you, including the right
      to complain to your local regulator.
    </p>

    <h2 id="children">Children</h2>
    <p>
      Musa CAD and this site aren’t directed at children, and I don’t knowingly collect anything
      from anyone, of any age.
    </p>

    <h2 id="changes">Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>
      If what the app or the site does with data changes, this page changes with it, and the date
      at the top moves. Every past version is in this website’s
      <a href="https://github.com/MusaCAD/MusaCAD-Website/commits/main/src/pages/privacy.astro" target="_blank" rel="noopener">public history</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Page>
